AB  - Multimodal retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) improves evidence-grounded question answering, but many existing systems still rely mainly on textual retrieval and weakly handle visual, OCR, table, and metadata evidence. This paper proposes a lightweight MLLM-driven multimodal RAG framework following an “Input-Retrieve-Fuse-Reason-Output” pipeline. Given a query, visual, textual, and OCR-based retrievers collect modality-specific candidates. A lightweight evidence fusion module then normalizes retrieval scores, considers query relevance and cross-modal alignment, penalizes redundancy, and selects a compact multimodal context for MLLM reasoning. Experiments on WebQA and MultiModalQA show consistent improvements over direct MLLM answering, text-only RAG, multimodal retrieval without fusion, score-normalized multimodal RAG, and cross-modal reranking multimodal RAG. The framework achieves 60.7/62.5/68.9/83.4 on WebQA and 69.2/71.4/79.3/89.6 on MultiModalQA in terms of EM/Acc./F1/ER, showing improved answer quality and evidence reliability.
