AB  - The rapid expansion of e-commerce platforms has increased the need for intelligent conversational agents capable of delivering accurate, context-aware product assistance. However, traditional rule-based and standard retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) chatbots struggle with relational reasoning and often generate hallucinated responses due to weak knowledge grounding. To address these limitations, this paper proposes an Adaptive Graph-Based Retrieval-Augmented Generation framework integrated with a Large Language Model (LLM) for ecommerce applications. A semantic product knowledge graph models structured relationships among products, attributes, and user preferences, enabling multi-hop relational retrieval with weighted relevance scoring. The retrieved subgraph is transformed into grounded context for controlled LLM generation. Experimental evaluation against rule-based and standard RAG baselines demonstrates improvements in response accuracy (91%), contextual relevance (89%), and significant hallucination reduction (4%). The proposed hybrid graph-LLM architecture provides a scalable and reliable solution for trustworthy conversational commerce systems.
