AB  - Large language models (LLMs) face significant challenges in knowledge-intensive tasks, including knowledge solidification, insufficient timeliness, and hallucination generation. To address these challenges, this study proposes an innovative retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) method based on dynamic knowledge indexing and semantic alignment optimization. However, traditional RAG methods suffer from semantic fragmentation and query-knowledge misalignment. The approach constructs a dynamic multi-granularity knowledge indexing system and integrates an intelligent dual-path routing decision mechanism to achieve efficient collaboration between knowledge retrieval and generation processes. Key innovations include: (1) a dynamic multi-granularity (comprising lexical-level inverted indexing and semantic-level vector indexing) knowledge indexing architecture that employs an adaptive chunking algorithm based on semantic coherence analysis to resolve semantic fragmentation caused by fixed-length segmentation; (2) a prompt engineering-driven semantic alignment optimization mechanism that unifies the semantic space of queries and knowledge bases by generating hypothetical question-answer pairs, significantly improving retrieval accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements: 37.01 EM and 47.53 F1 on HotpotQA, 35.12 EM and 45.69 F1 on SQuAD. This work provides a reliable technical solution for intelligent question answering and advances RAG technology in semantic understanding and retrieval accuracy.
