AB  - Web server logs play a crucial role in failure diagnosis, performance monitoring, and anomaly detection, yet the large volume and semi-structured nature of log data render manual analysis inefficient and difficult to scale. This paper presents an intelligent log analysis framework based on Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) integrated with a large language model to support contextual question answering over NGINX web server logs. NGINX access logs and official configuration documentation are encoded into semantic vector representations and stored in separate vector databases. Relevant log entries and documentation fragments are retrieved in response to user queries and refined through a cross-encoder-based re-ranking mechanism and subsequently provided as contextual input to a large language model for answer generation. System performance is evaluated using BERTScore metrics across five representative log analysis questions assessed by ten participants with professional experience in NGINX administration. Experimental results indicate that re-ranking improves semantic alignment, increasing the average BERTScore F1 from 84.57% to 87.91% across all ten respondents. These findings demonstrate that re-ranking enhances contextual relevance and response quality in LLM-based log analysis, enabling more accurate diagnostics for web server operations.
