AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) adds external knowledge to large language models (LLMs) to reduce hallucinations and outdated knowledge. But because the retriever is mostly fixed, performance drops when user questions and the knowledge change, letting retrieval mistakes spread through the system while the useful feedback in existing workflow goes largely unused. To this end, we introduce LUF, a self-improving framework that leverages implicit feedback from both LLMs and users to continuously adapt. LUF uses three complementary training objectives—strict supervision from answers, soft supervision from related content, and hard negative penalization—to guide updates to the retriever and document database, without requiring explicit annotations. Experiments on question answering tasks show that LUF significantly improves both retriever accuracy and LLM response quality, providing more accurate answers compared to baselines.
