AB  - Accessing, gathering, and understanding historical information is quite difficult because it comes from limited, fragmented, handwritten, and multi-language resources. This research was conducted as part of an effort to help access information about historical things. We choose Sumedang Larang Kingdom as the object of study because it is part of Sundanese culture. Most hybrid QA research has been focused on the industrial and technical fields, and most only involve a combination of two components. This research is one of the first that uses Retrieval-Augmented Generation, Knowledge Graph, and Ontology in the field of culture and history. In this study, we present a hybrid semantic question answering (QA) system that combines structured and unstructured knowledge to provide an answer that is more accurate and contextually aware. The system was evaluated using a collection of 100 diverse historical questions, where the hybrid approach reached an accuracy of 81% and outperformed the individual knowledge components. These results highlight the potential complementarity of different knowledge sources and the feasibility of hybrid QA approaches in sensitive historical domains. In addition to its technical aspects, this work also represents a contribution to cultural preservation and digital education, as it seeks to bridge advanced natural language processing techniques with the historical knowledge of the local community.
ER  - 

