AB  - This study introduces Sanity-LLM, an advanced Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework for mental health chatbots that aims to produce responses that are accurate, empathetic, and safe. The proposed architecture integrates hybrid semantic–keyword search, step-back questioning, and a reranking mechanism to improve the relevance and grounding of generated responses. The knowledge base was built from publicly available mental health dialogues and Q&A data, embedded and indexed using FAISS. Evaluation using RAGAS metrics demonstrates notable performance improvements, achieving 0.9391 context precision, 0.7077 context recall, 0.8754 faithfulness, and 0.6297 answer correctness, surpassing naïve RAG baselines. Ablation results further confirm that each module: hybrid retrieval, step-back prompting, and reranking contributes significantly to the overall system effectiveness. While not a substitute for professional care, Sanity-LLM offers a scalable and accessible first-line support tool for encouraging help-seeking behavior. Future work will focus on broadening verified knowledge sources and validating effectiveness through expert and user-based evaluation.
