AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate formidable reasoning capabilities yet remain constrained by static parametric knowledge. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates this limitation by integrating external knowledge retrieval, thereby enhancing adaptability and factual accuracy. Nevertheless, existing RAG systems suffer from hallucinations, inefficient knowledge fusion, and computational overhead. This paper introduces ModuRAG, an adaptive retrieval-augmented generation framework leveraging small models that dynamically optimizes retrieval and response generation through four pivotal modules: self-knowledge, task decomposition, passage relevance assessment, and answer reflection generation modules. By harnessing fine-tuned compact models, ModuRAG enhances retrieval precision, reduces redundant computation, and improves multi-step reasoning efficiency. Experiments conducted across multiple open-domain question-answering datasets demonstrate that ModuRAG achieves superior accuracy while reducing response latency, outperforming existing benchmarks. Notably, its adaptive mechanism substantially elevates performance in complex reasoning tasks. Future endeavors will concentrate on optimizing resource efficiency and expanding real-time applicability in dynamic knowledge environments.
ER  - 

