AB  - Current medical chatbots often lack credibility, personalization, and contextual awareness, which limits their reliability in delivering accurate healthcare guidance. They typically provide generic responses without considering individual health records, thereby increasing the risk of misinformation. This paper presents a credible and personalized healthcare assistant powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) that generates context-aware, user-specific medical responses. The system integrates Optical Character Recognition (OCR) to extract structured data from user-uploaded prescriptions and diagnostic reports, which are stored in a database. A Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture is employed to enhance response accuracy by combining user-specific data with relevant knowledge retrieved from external sources, specifically the Gale Encyclopedia of Medicine, represented as embeddings in a vector database. Queries are processed using the Mistral model, which leverages both user data and retrieved context to produce accurate and trustworthy medical insights. By uniting OCR-based document understanding, user-centric data modeling, and external knowledge retrieval, the proposed system provides reliable, scalable, and personalized healthcare support.
