AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) ensures that the factual detail in domain-specific question answering is grounded in external documents, thus improving the factual accuracy of LLM responses. Current RAG systems, however, do not have transparency in retrieval decisions, lack faithfulness verification, and inefficiently rely on the same static similarity thresholds for all prior retrievals. In this paper, an Explainable and Faithfulness-Aware RAG framework with Adaptive Semantic Caching is proposed. The framework consists of a hybrid faithfulness metric that combines the three criteria: semantic similarity, keyword overlap, and named-entity consistency, along with a dynamic caching threshold that changes according to the length of the query, the length of the query history, the domain sensitivity, and the intent of the query. The results in healthcare (heart disease), legal (CUAD) and open-domain (SQuAD v2) datasets show that it achieves higher cache precision, lower latency and better faithfulness grounding than static-caching and semantic-only baselines. The contribution of each component is confirmed by ablation studies.
