AB  - Currently, medical question answering for aging populations often suffers from mismatched retrieval scope and unverifiable answers, making it difficult to balance clinical rigor with understandability. To address these issues, we construct a medical knowledge graph from multi-source data (electronic health records, clinical guidelines, biomedical literature, and drug knowledge bases) and enhance LLM answering via KG-based retrieval. Specifically, community constraints are used to identify a local sub-graph relevant to the query, employ TOG beam search to organize multi-hop evidence chains, and design a structured prompting scheme that follows an "evidence-first, terminology-consistent, definite conclusion with reasonable inference" paradigm, thereby aligning geriatric-oriented medical answers with explicit paths on the graph. Experimental results on a self-constructed geriatric medical question answering dataset show that the proposed framework achieves the best overall performance among representative baselines, including general-purpose and specialized LLMs (DeepSeek-V3, GPT-4, ChatDoctor) and retrieval-augmented methods (BM25-RAG, embedding-RAG, and direct KG retriever), in terms of BERTScore, GPT-4 Rating, and first-token latency under a plug-and-play setting.
