AB  - Long-standing organizations rely heavily on historical documents rich in domain-specific terminology, yet manual retrieval remains slow and error-prone, while standard Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems often struggle with ambiguous jargon, abbreviations, and evolving terminology. To address these challenges, this paper presents a Knowledge Graph-based RAG framework augmented with a glossary-driven semantic interpretation layer designed to improve the accuracy and efficiency of domain-specific question answering. A usermaintained glossary with fuzzy matching enriches the retrieval context by providing precise definitions alongside knowledge graph triples, enabling the LLM to better interpret specialized terminology. The system was evaluated on publicly available realworld clinical trial protocol and a curated set of 30 domain questions. The system achieves a mean context recall of 0.839 and faithfulness of 0.933, confirming accurate graph-grounded retrieval. Glossary integration improved answer relevancy by 48.4% (0.483 → 0.717), The proposed lightweight, interpretable mechanism significantly enhances domain-specific information extraction and offers a scalable solution for preserving institutional knowledge across regulatory, clinical, and legal archives.
