AB  - This study presents the design and implementation of a curriculum-aligned question-answering agent tailored for educational applications, leveraging Retrieval-Augmented Generation with local deployment of large language models. The system integrates domain-specific instructional data-including course handbooks, program learning outcomes, and class schedules-into a Chroma vector database, enabling semantic retrieval via embedding-based similarity search. LLaMA 3.1, deployed locally through Ollama, is used as the language generation model. The framework is implemented using the LangChain orchestration library and exposed to users through the LINE messaging platform, facilitating mobile-friendly interaction in real-time. A benchmark dataset of 200 curriculum-related frequently asked questions was compiled and annotated to evaluate system performance. Experimental results show an accuracy of 90% on the training subset and 80% on the held-out test set. ChatGPT40 (OpenAI) was used as the baseline for comparative evaluation, and error analysis highlights challenges with ambiguous queries and long-range dependencies. Performance profiling reveals inference latency of approximately 2.1 seconds per response on consumer-grade hardware. This work demonstrates the feasibility and effectiveness of deploying lightweight yet accurate QA agents using open-source LLMs in constrained environments.
